---
import Leaderboard from '../components/Leaderboard.astro';
import SubscribePanel from '../components/SubscribePanel.astro';
import { analysis, repositories } from '../config/site';
import '../styles/site.css';

const windowDays = analysis.windowDays;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer" />
    <link rel="icon" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/favicon.svg`.replace(/\/{2,}/g, '/')} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="GitHub Discussions engagement metrics and Atom subscriptions, measured live from the GitHub API."
    />
    <title>Discussion engagement</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <header class="page-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Prototype</p>
        <h1>Discussion engagement</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          How busy a project's discussion board is, who is taking part, and how quickly
          questions get answered. Measured over the last {windowDays} days, live from the
          GitHub API. Subscribe to follow a board without checking this page.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="access" aria-labelledby="access-heading">
        <div class="access-copy">
          <h2 id="access-heading">Load live metrics</h2>
          <p>
            GitHub's GraphQL API requires a token, including for public discussions. This
            site has no server and ships no credential: supply your own read-only token.
            It is held in memory, sent only to api.github.com, and never written to disk
            unless you tick the box below.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            A classic token with no scopes selected, or a fine-grained token with
            read-only access to public repositories, is enough. Do not paste a token that
            carries write access.
          </p>
        </div>

        <form id="access-form">
          <label for="github-token">GitHub token</label>
          <input
            id="github-token"
            name="token"
            type="password"
            autocomplete="off"
            spellcheck="false"
            required
            placeholder="ghp_… or github_pat_…"
          />
          <label class="checkbox">
            <input type="checkbox" id="remember" />
            Keep it for this browser tab only
          </label>
          <div class="form-actions">
            <button type="submit" id="load">Load metrics</button>
            <button type="button" class="secondary" id="cancel" hidden>Cancel</button>
          </div>
          <p class="form-status" id="form-status" role="status">Waiting for a token.</p>
        </form>

        <noscript>
          <p class="notice">
            Metrics need JavaScript, because the browser calls the GitHub API directly.
            The subscription links below work without it.
          </p>
        </noscript>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="leaderboard-heading">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <h2 id="leaderboard-heading">Leaderboard</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            One row per enrolled repository. Ranking, filtering, and org-wide totals
            arrive with the multi-repository pass.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Leaderboard repositories={repositories} />
      </section>

      {repositories.map((repository) => <SubscribePanel repository={repository} />)}

      <section class="methodology" aria-labelledby="methodology-heading">
        <h2 id="methodology-heading">How these numbers are produced</h2>

        <p>
          The browser reads the discussion board through the GitHub GraphQL API each time
          you load metrics. Nothing is cached, stored, or precomputed at build time, so
          every figure reflects the board as it stands at that moment.
        </p>

        <h3>Computed from a single fetch</h3>
        <p>
          Every metric on this page, including the sparkline and the trend, is derived
          from timestamps that the API returns alongside each discussion, comment, and
          reply. Because each contribution carries its own <code>createdAt</code>, one
          fetch is enough to reconstruct when activity happened and to compare one period
          against another. No snapshot history is required.
        </p>

        <h3>What would need stored history</h3>
        <p>
          Metrics about state that GitHub only reports as it stands today. Whether a
          question was answered is a current flag; a question answered last week is
          indistinguishable from one answered a year ago except where GitHub also exposes
          an <code>answerChosenAt</code> timestamp. Contributions that were deleted, and
          discussions moved between categories, leave no trace to recover. Measuring those
          over time needs periodic snapshots, which this prototype does not keep.
        </p>

        <h3>Definitions</h3>
        <dl class="definitions">
          <dt>Window</dt>
          <dd>The {windowDays} days ending when the fetch starts.</dd>

          <dt>Participants</dt>
          <dd>
            Distinct accounts that opened a discussion, commented, or replied within the
            window. Bots count. Deleted accounts cannot be attributed and are excluded.
          </dd>

          <dt>Comments</dt>
          <dd>
            Comments and threaded replies created within the window, including those on
            discussions opened long before it.
          </dd>

          <dt>Answered</dt>
          <dd>
            The share of discussions opened in the window, in categories that accept
            answers, that currently have an accepted answer. Categories that do not accept
            answers are excluded rather than counted as unanswered.
          </dd>

          <dt>First response</dt>
          <dd>
            Median time between a discussion opening and the first contribution from
            somebody other than its author. Discussions with no such response are not
            counted, so this measures the speed of answers given, not the odds of getting
            one.
          </dd>

          <dt>New and returning</dt>
          <dd>
            A participant is new when the window holds their first contribution to this
            board. This needs the whole board to be read; if the fetch stops early, the
            metric reports insufficient history rather than guessing.
          </dd>

          <dt>Trend</dt>
          <dd>
            Total contributions in the window against the immediately preceding window of
            equal length.
          </dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>When a number is missing</h3>
        <p>
          A cell without a number never means zero. It says which of three things
          happened. <span class="state-word">No activity</span> means the window was read
          successfully and nothing happened in it.
          <span class="state-word">Insufficient history</span> means the board was read,
          but not far enough back to answer that particular question, usually because the
          request budget or the rate limit ran out.
          <span class="state-word">No data</span> means the fetch failed, so the metric
          was never measured. A failure affecting one metric leaves the others, and every
          subscription link, intact.
        </p>
      </section>

      <footer class="page-footer">
        <p>
          Enrolled repositories come from a single configuration file. Sourcing that list
          from the CNCF Landscape is a data change, not a code change.
        </p>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script>
      import '../scripts/dashboard';
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
